Chapter: Trig

 Fixed-point trigonometric approximations safe for real-time
 contexts. Every function is punctual: no heap, no recursion,
 bounded instruction count.

 All angles are in millidegrees (1 degree = 1000 units) to avoid
 floating-point. Results are scaled by 10000 (so 10000 = 1.0,
 -10000 = -1.0). This gives ~0.01% precision, sufficient for
 motor control, sensor fusion, and embedded graphics.

 We say:

Section: Angle Normalization

  punctual trig-normalize : Integer -> Integer
  trig-normalize (mdeg) =
    let m = mdeg - mdeg / 360000 * 360000
    in if m < 0 then m + 360000 else m

Section: Sine (3rd-order Bhaskara I approximation)

  punctual fast-sin : Integer -> Integer
  fast-sin (mdeg) =
    let a = trig-normalize mdeg
    in let flip = a >= 180000
    in let b = if flip then a - 180000 else a
    in let num = 4 * b * (180000 - b)
    in let den = 40500000000 - b * (180000 - b)
    in let r = num * 10000 / den
    in if flip then 0 - r else r

Section: Cosine

  punctual fast-cos : Integer -> Integer
  fast-cos (mdeg) = fast-sin (mdeg + 90000)

Section: Combined Sin + Cos

  punctual fast-sin-cos-sum : Integer -> Integer
  fast-sin-cos-sum (mdeg) = fast-sin mdeg + fast-cos mdeg

Section: Unit Conversion

  punctual deg-to-mrad : Integer -> Integer
  deg-to-mrad (mdeg) = mdeg * 1745 / 100000

  punctual mrad-to-deg : Integer -> Integer
  mrad-to-deg (mrad) = mrad * 100000 / 1745

